# First Principles

A Finding:

- Explains.
- Summarizes.
- Interprets.
- Is defensible.
- Exists independently of Recommendations.

A Finding does not:

- Recommend.
- Predict.
- Execute.
- Decide.

---

# Relationship to Evidence

Evidence supports Findings.

Findings cannot exist without Evidence.

One Evidence package may contribute to multiple Findings.

Multiple Evidence packages may support one Finding.

---

# Relationship to Recommendations

Findings identify reality.

Recommendations propose change.

Finding

↓

Recommendation

Reality is understood before improvement is proposed.

---

# Relationship to Executive Decisions

Executives do not decide directly from Evidence.

Executives reason through Findings.

Findings translate structured Evidence into business language.

---

# Candidate Characteristics

Every Finding is:

- Explainable
- Evidence-backed
- Organization-scoped
- Assessment-scoped
- Traceable
- Defensible
- Immutable once Assessment closes

---

# Candidate Lifecycle

Evidence Forged

↓

Evidence Evaluated

↓

Finding Produced

↓

Finding Reviewed

↓

Finding Approved

↓

Available for Recommendation Generation

↓

Archived with Assessment

---

# Constitutional Invariants

Every Finding:

- References one or more Evidence packages.
- Belongs to one Assessment.
- Belongs to one Organization.
- Can be independently audited.
- Cannot exist without Evidence.
- Exists before Recommendations.

---

# Relationship to Trust

Executives trust Findings because every Finding can be traced back to Evidence, and every Evidence package can be traced back to Observations.

Observation

↓

Evidence

↓

Finding

Trust increases through complete explainability.

---

